The answer is yes. This solution takes advantage of the fact that  ${10 \choose 2} + {11 \choose 2} + \cdots + {14 \choose 2} = 335 = 5 \cdot 67$ .

Let  $S_i$  denote the set of singers that singer  $i$  prefers to appear after. Consider the construction:
\begin{align*}
S_1 &= \emptyset 
S_2 &= \{1\} 
\vdots & \vdots 
S_6 &= \{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\} 
S_7 &= \{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\} 
S_8 &= \{7\} 
S_9 &= \{8\} 
S_{10} &= \{9\} 
S_{11} &= \{6\} 
S_{12} &= \{11\} 
S_{13} &= \{1, 2, 3, \dots, 12\} 
S_{14} &= \{13\} 
\vdots & \vdots 
S_{18} &= \{17\} 
S_{19} &= \{17\} 
S_{20} &= \{17\}.
\end{align*}
Notice that in this construction, the numbers  $1, 2, 3, 4, 5$  must appear first in that order. Now to decipher what is going on, first notice that singers  $\{18, 19, 20\}$  must appear at the end of the chain and are indistinguishable otherwise, so we assume they appear in this order, dropping a factor of  $6$ .

The key is to consider the *side chain* consisting of  $\{7, 8, 9, 10\}$ . We have five cases here:


- Singer  $6$  appears in position  $6$ . Then the singers  $7, 8, 9, 10, 13, \dots, 20$  must appear in this order and singer  $12$  must appear before singer  $11$ . There are  ${14 \choose 2}$  ways to choose positions for singers  $11$  and  $12$ , and the positions of the remaining singers are then fixed.
- Singer  $6$  appears in position  $7$ . The only singer that can appear in position  $6$  is singer  $7$ , and the remaining  $13$  positions work out similarly, with  ${13 \choose 2}$  ways to pick positions for singers  $11$  and  $12$  and the positions of the remaining singers being fixed.
- Singer  $6$  appears in position  $8$ . Once again, only singer  $7$  can appear in position  $6$ , and only singer  $8$  can appear in position  $7$ . By the same logic, we have  ${12 \choose 2}$  cases.
- If singer  $6$  appears in position  $9$  or  $10$ , we have  ${11 \choose 2}$  and  ${10 \choose 2}$  cases respectively.
- Singer  $6$  cannot appear in position  $11$  or later, as all of singers  $11$  through  $20$  must appear after singer  $6$ .

Thus, multiplying back by the factor of  $6$ , there are  $6 \cdot 335 = 2010$  ways to pick the order of the singers, as needed.**<span style="color:purple">Remark:**</span> Pretty hard for C1, but awesome problem.

Well, such an antiproblem for  $C1$   ;) **Solution:** It can happen.
    For convenience, let  $X\rightarrow Y$  iff  $X$  comes before  $Y$ , so that we can connect restrictions  $X\rightarrow Y \rightarrow Z \rightarrow\dots$ . 
    Now, let  $b_1,b_2,b_3, A,B,C,D,E,a_1,a_2,\dots a_{12}$  be the  $20$  singers. We claim the following satisfies:
    \[\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
         b_1\rightarrow b_2 \rightarrow b_3 \rightarrow A,B,C,D,E, a_1,a_2,\dots a_{12}  
         A\rightarrow B,C,E
         D\rightarrow C \rightarrow E
         A,B,C,D\rightarrow a_1 \rightarrow a_2 \rightarrow \dots \rightarrow a_7 \rightarrow a_8,a_9,a_{10}, a_{11},a_{12}
         a_{8}\rightarrow a_{9}
         a_{10} \rightarrow a_{11}
    \end{array}\right.\]
    First, note that  $b_1$ ,  $b_2$  and  $b_3$  are fixed. Furthermore, the next 4 spots are elements of  $\{A,B,C,D,E\}$ . 
    If those 4 spots and the next one (so from the fourth to the eighth) are precisely  $\{A,B,C,D,E\}$ , there are  $7$  ways to place them.
    Besides, if they aren't, then the four spots we previously mentioned are  $\{A,B,C,D\}$  and there're  $5$  ways to place them, but now  $E$  will have  $13$  possibilities.
    Finally, the end is composed of  $a_1,a_2,a_3\dots a_{7}$  in that order and finally  $a_8,a_9,a_{10},a_{11},a_{12}$  in some order (there're  $\frac{5!}{2\cdot 2}=30$  ways to order them), hence, for this order is independent to the choices of  $A,B,C,D$  and  $E$ , we get that the number of ways to order the singers is  $30(7+12\cdot 5)=2\cdot 3\cdot 5\cdot 67=2010$

