We set the circle centered at  $M$  with radius  $MA$  be the unit circle. We know that this circle passes through the points  $B$  ,  $P$  and  $Q$ . WLOG rotate so that  $a=1$  and thus  $b=-1$ . Now, using the chord intersection formula, we calculate 
\begin{align*}
t &= \frac{ab(p+q)-pq(a+b)}{ab-pq}
&= \frac{p+q}{pq+1}
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
c &= \frac{aq(b+p)-bp(a+q)}{aq-bp}
&= \frac{2pq+p-q}{p+q}
\end{align*}
and 
\begin{align*}
h &= \frac{ap(b+p)}{aq-bp}
&= \frac{2pq +q-p}{p+q}
\end{align*}

Now, we simply note that 
\begin{align*}
\frac{c-m}{t-h} &= \frac{\frac{2pq+p-q}{p+q} - 0}{\frac{p+q}{pq+1} - \frac{2pq +q-p}{p+q}}
&= \frac{(2pq+p-q)(pq+1)}{p^2+q^2+p-q+p^2q-pq^2-2p^2q^2}
&= \frac{(2pq+p-q)(pq+1)}{-(2pq+p-q)(pq-p+q-1)}
&= - \frac{pq+1}{pq-p+q-1}
\end{align*}
Now the path is clear. Simply note that 
\begin{align*}
\overline{\left(\frac{c-m}{t-h}\right)} &= \overline{\left(- \frac{pq+1}{pq-p+q-1}\right)}
&= -\frac{\frac{1}{pq}+1}{\frac{1}{pq}-\frac{1}{p}+\frac{1}{q}+1}
&= - \frac{\frac{pq+1}{pq}}{\frac{1-q+p-pq}{pq}}
&= \frac{pq+1}{pq-p+q-1}
\end{align*}
from which it is clear that  $\frac{c-m}{t-h} = -\overline{\left(\frac{c-m}{t-h}\right)}$ . Thus,  $\frac{c-m}{t-h} \in i\mathbb{R}$  which proves that  $CM \perp TH$  as desired.

