solution_md:
I'm assuming that we can use calculators.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let the side length of the square be  $a$ , and let  $EP=y$  and thus  $PF=a-y$ .  We cal also find the measure of  $BF$ :
\[
\tan(18)=\frac{BF}{a}\implies BF=a\tan(18)\implies FC=a[1-\tan(18)]
\]
Since  $[PFCA]=2[AEPG]$ , we have
\begin{align*}
(a-y)a[1-\tan(18)]&=2(y)[a\tan(18)] 
(a-y)[1-\tan(18)]&=2(y)\tan(18) 
\frac{a-y}{y}&=\frac{2\tan(18)}{1-\tan(18)} 
\frac{a}{y}-1&=\frac{2\tan(18)}{1-\tan(18)} 
\frac{a}{y}&=\frac{2\tan(18)+1-\tan(18)}{1-\tan(18)} 
\frac{1}{y}&=\frac{1+\tan(18)}{a[1-\tan(18)]} 
y&=a\left[\frac{1-\tan(18)}{1+\tan(18)}\right]
\end{align*}

Call the point in which  $AF$  intersects  $GH$  point  $X$  and the intersection of  $AH$  and  $EF$  point  $Y$ .

Then we know that  $AXH$  make a triangle and  $\angle AXH=18+90=108$  because it is an exterior angle.  Thus, if we know  $\angle XHA$ , then  $x$  would come out easily.  To find that angle, let us find the length of  $PY$  so we can use the tangent function to find that angle.

By similar triangles,  $PY=y\left\{\frac{\cancel{a}[1-\tan(18)]}{\cancel{a}}\right\}=y\left([1-\tan(18)]\right)$ .

Thus, using the tangent function:
\begin{align*}
\tan(XHA)&=\frac{y[1-\tan(18)]}{a[1-\tan(18)]} 
\tan(XHA)&=\cancel{a}\left[\frac{\cancel{1-\tan(18)}}{1+\tan(18)}\right]\left\{\frac{[1-\tan(18)]}{\cancel{a}\cancel{[1-\tan(18)]}}\right\} 
\tan(XHA)&=\frac{1-\tan(18)}{1+\tan(18)}
\arctan(\tan(XHA))&=\arctan\left(\frac{1-\tan(18)}{1+\tan(18)}\right) 
\angle XHA&=27^\circ
\end{align*}
[Turns out nicely...](http://www.wolframalpha.com/input/?i=arctan%28%281-tan%2818%29%29%2F%281%2Btan%2818%29%29%29)
Thus,  $x=180-108-27=\boxed{45^\circ}$ .</details>

