solution_md:
Let the sum of numbers in each  box to be  $\mathcal{S}$  then  $k\cdot \mathcal{S}=\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$  so  $k=\frac{n(n+1)}{2\mathcal{S}}$  we want to maximize  $k \implies$  we want to to minimise  $\mathcal{S}$ .

Now:




- $n \equiv 1 \pmod 2$ :


Then clearly we also know that  $\mathcal{S} \geqslant n$  so for minimum   $\mathcal{S}$  we take  $\mathcal{S}$  to be  $n$  in that case we get  $k=\frac{n+1}{2} \in \mathbb{Z}^{+}$  hence this works and the construction works as:  $\{n\}, \{1,n-1\} , \{2,n-2\} , \cdots , \{\frac{n-1}{2} , \frac{n+1}{2}\}$ .
- $n \equiv 0 \pmod 2$ :

Then clearly we also know that  $\mathcal{S} \geqslant n+1$  as taking  $\mathcal{S}=n$  won't give us  $k$  to be integer , hence we set $\mathcal{S}=n+1$  , so  $k=\frac{n}{2}$  which works , and the construction is as follows :  $\{1, n\}, \{2, n-1\} , \{3, n-2\} , \cdots , \{ \frac{n}{2} , \frac{n}{2}+1\}$  .


Hence In all the answer is\[
\boxed{\max(k)=
\begin{cases}
\frac{n+1}{2}, & \text{if } n \equiv 1 \pmod{2} 
\frac{n}{2}, & \text{if } n \equiv 0 \pmod{2}
\end{cases}
}
\]  $\blacksquare$

