solution_md:
Suppose that  $\displaystyle\int_0^{\infty}\frac{f(x)-f(x+1)}{f(x)}dx$  converges. Let  $\epsilon>0$ . By the Cauchy criterion, there exists  $R>0$  such that  $b>a>R$  implies  $\displaystyle\int_a^b\frac{f(x)-f(x+1)}{f(x)}dx<\frac{\epsilon}{2}$ .

Fix  $a>R$ . Since  $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}f(x)=0$ , there exists  $T>0$  such that  $b>T$  implies  $f(b)<\frac{f(a)\epsilon}{2}$ . Then if  $b>\max\{a,T\}$ ,
\begin{align*}
	\frac{\epsilon}{2}&>\int_a^b\frac{f(x)-f(x+1)}{f(x)}dx
	&\geq\frac{1}{f(a)}\int_a^bf(x)-f(x+1)dx
	&=\frac{1}{f(a)}\int_a^{a+1}f(x)dx-\frac{1}{f(a)}\int_b^{b+1}f(x)dx
	&\geq\frac{f(a+1)}{f(a)}-\frac{f(b)}{f(a)}
	&>\frac{f(a+1)}{f(a)}-\frac{\epsilon}{2}
\end{align*}
so  $1>\frac{f(a)-f(a+1)}{f(a)}>1-\epsilon$ .

Thus for all  $\epsilon>0$ , there exists an  $R>0$  such that  $a>R$  implies  $\left|\frac{f(a)-f(a+1)}{f(a)}-1\right|<\epsilon$ , so  $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{f(x)-f(x+1)}{f(x)}=1$ . But  $\displaystyle\int_0^{\infty}\frac{f(x)-f(x+1)}{f(x)}dx$  converges, so  $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{f(x)-f(x+1)}{f(x)}=0$ , contradiction. Thus  $\displaystyle\int_0^{\infty}\frac{f(x)-f(x+1)}{f(x)}dx$  does not converge. Since the integrand is positive, the integral diverges.

